The user just ran /insights to generate a usage report analyzing their Claude Code sessions.

Here is the full insights data:
{
  "project_areas": {
    "areas": [
      {
        "name": "PR Review Resolution & Code Quality",
        "session_count": 35,
        "description": "The dominant workflow involved triaging and resolving automated PR review comments from CodeRabbit and Copilot across multiple rounds. Claude fixed flagged issues across TypeScript and Rust files, replied to and resolved review threads, and performed code simplification tasks like extracting shared helpers and removing dead code. This was a highly repetitive, essential workflow that drove the majority of sessions."
      },
      {
        "name": "Phased Project Planning & Execution (GSD Workflow)",
        "session_count": 25,
        "description": "The user employed a structured \"GSD\" planning system with phased development, where Claude researched, planned, verified, and executed implementation plans across numbered phases (11-19). Work included Recycle Bin feature planning, IPFS infrastructure milestone scoping, gap-closure phase creation, milestone audits, and progress tracking. Claude also maintained project state files, roadmaps, and planning artifacts."
      },
      {
        "name": "CI/CD Pipeline & GitHub Actions",
        "session_count": 18,
        "description": "Significant effort went into fixing CI failures, consolidating desktop CI/E2E workflows, adding Rust test coverage to pipelines, debugging platform-specific build issues (macOS pkg-config, Windows FUSE, Linux xvfb), and implementing features like migration drift checks and release gates. This area had the most friction, with iterative debugging cycles across multiple platforms and repeated CI fix rounds."
      },
      {
        "name": "Feature Implementation & Bug Fixes",
        "session_count": 10,
        "description": "Claude implemented features including version CID cleanup, replace-file-on-duplicate-upload with tests, security fixes, and tech debt resolution. Work spanned TypeScript and Rust codebases with Claude writing new tests (50+ in one session), debugging failures, and ensuring test coverage met Codecov thresholds."
      },
      {
        "name": "Git Operations & Project Housekeeping",
        "session_count": 7,
        "description": "Sessions focused on git operations like branch management, merging PRs, pulling latest changes, disk cleanup, and project maintenance tasks such as archiving completed phases, creating retroactive summaries, enforcing PR naming conventions, and updating STATE.md. Claude also helped with README restructuring planning and documentation updates."
      }
    ]
  },
  "interaction_style": {
    "narrative": "You operate with a **heavily automated, workflow-driven approach** to development, relying on custom commands like `/gsd:progress`, `/gsd:plan-phase`, and `resolve-pr-reviews` to orchestrate Claude through structured multi-step processes. Your 100 sessions over a month show a clear pattern: you trigger automated workflows, let Claude execute across multiple files and tools, then intervene surgically when something goes wrong. With **294 commits across 95 sessions** and Bash as your dominant tool (3,409 invocations), you're clearly letting Claude do the heavy lifting on execution while you maintain strategic oversight. Your top goals — resolving PR reviews (54 sessions), git operations (28), and fixing CI failures (13) — reveal someone who has **built an entire development pipeline around Claude as an autonomous executor**.\n\nWhat's distinctive is how you **catch errors that Claude misses and course-correct decisively**. You noticed when Claude incorrectly dismissed a valid CodeRabbit comment, when it accidentally destroyed M1 milestone docs during renaming, when it committed to already-merged branches (multiple times), and when it reported threads as resolved that clearly weren't. You interrupted Claude twice specifically when it was going down the wrong path instead of listening to your preferred approach. The friction data tells the story: 43 instances of wrong approach and 34 of buggy code, yet your satisfaction remains overwhelmingly positive (310 satisfied/likely-satisfied vs 20 negative) with 86 of 95 sessions fully or mostly achieved. You tolerate Claude's mistakes because your workflow is built to catch them — you review, redirect, and re-trigger until it's right. Your interaction with the TypeScript/Rust/YAML codebase across milestone planning, phase execution, PR creation, and review resolution shows someone managing a **complex multi-milestone project where Claude serves as your primary implementation layer**.",
    "key_pattern": "You've built a structured command-driven workflow system where Claude autonomously executes multi-step development phases while you provide strategic oversight and surgical corrections when it drifts."
  },
  "what_works": {
    "intro": "Over the past month, you've run 100 sessions with a 90%+ success rate, driving a highly structured phased development workflow across TypeScript and Rust codebases.",
    "impressive_workflows": [
      {
        "title": "Automated PR Review Resolution Pipeline",
        "description": "You've built a remarkably efficient loop around resolving PR review comments — over half your sessions involve triggering resolve-pr-reviews commands that triage, fix, and resolve threads in bulk. You're routinely clearing 15-19 review threads per session across Rust and TypeScript, turning code review from a bottleneck into a streamlined process."
      },
      {
        "title": "Structured Multi-Phase Project Execution",
        "description": "You're running a sophisticated phased development system (GSD) where Claude handles research, planning, verification with revision loops, and execution across numbered phases. Your workflow includes milestone audits, gap-closure phases, and retroactive summaries — giving you strong project governance while still moving fast through 294 commits in a month."
      },
      {
        "title": "Multi-File Changes at Scale",
        "description": "Sixty of your sessions involved successful multi-file changes, and you consistently trust Claude with large cross-cutting edits like extracting shared helpers across 4 files, adding 50+ tests in one session, or restructuring CI workflows. Your heavy use of Bash (3,400+ calls) alongside Edit and Read shows you're verifying changes end-to-end rather than just generating code."
      }
    ]
  },
  "friction_analysis": {
    "intro": "Your most significant friction patterns revolve around Claude taking wrong approaches—especially around branch/file targeting mistakes and iterative CI debugging loops that consume excessive time.",
    "categories": [
      {
        "category": "Wrong branch and file targeting",
        "description": "You repeatedly hit issues where Claude committed to already-merged branches or edited global files instead of project-local ones, despite having context that should have prevented this. Being more explicit about the target branch/path upfront, or asking Claude to confirm the current branch before any commit, could save correction rounds.",
        "examples": [
          "Claude committed to an already-merged branch twice, even though it had a memory note about checking the current branch first, requiring manual cleanup",
          "Claude edited the global GSD install instead of your project-local copy and used --global flag incorrectly, causing multiple rounds of correction including a broken statusline config"
        ]
      },
      {
        "category": "Iterative CI debugging spirals",
        "description": "A disproportionate amount of your sessions involve multi-round CI failure debugging where Claude's initial fixes are incomplete, leading to cascading fix attempts. Consider having Claude analyze the full CI log and propose a comprehensive fix plan before pushing incremental attempts.",
        "examples": [
          "Desktop E2E CI fixes required multiple rounds—first round didn't properly fix macOS wget, Linux xvfb quoting, or Windows POST issues, and the second round only revealed deeper app-level failures",
          "Claude spent extensive time debugging a ts-node decorator compilation error, trying multiple approaches (reflect-metadata import, require flags) before finally finding the correct --project tsconfig.json fix"
        ]
      },
      {
        "category": "Dismissing valid feedback and missing context",
        "description": "Claude sometimes confidently dismissed valid review comments or lost important context from prior sessions, requiring you to manually intervene and correct course. Prompting Claude to double-check its assumptions before dismissing external feedback could reduce these incidents.",
        "examples": [
          "Claude incorrectly dismissed a valid CodeRabbit comment about publishBatch needing instrumentation, falsely claiming it delegated to publishRecord when it didn't—you had to catch and correct this",
          "Claude reported all PR review threads as resolved when you could see unresolved 'outside of diff range' comments still visible in a screenshot"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "suggestions": {
    "claude_md_additions": [
      {
        "addition": "Always check the current branch status before committing. Run `git status` and `git branch` to confirm you're on the correct, non-merged branch before any commit/push operation.",
        "why": "Claude committed to already-merged branches multiple times across sessions, despite even having a memory about this — it needs to be a hard rule in CLAUDE.md.",
        "prompt_scaffold": "Add under a ## Git Operations section at the top level"
      },
      {
        "addition": "When resolving PR review comments, verify ALL threads are actually resolved by checking the PR page — do not report 'all resolved' based solely on your own actions. Comments marked 'outside of diff range' still need attention.",
        "why": "Claude prematurely reported all threads resolved when unresolved comments were still visible, and this pattern of PR review resolution is the #1 workflow (54+ sessions).",
        "prompt_scaffold": "Add under a ## PR Reviews section"
      },
      {
        "addition": "When editing project files, always modify the project-local copy, never the global/system install. Double-check file paths before editing configuration or tooling files.",
        "why": "Claude edited global GSD install instead of project-local files, used --global flags incorrectly, causing multiple rounds of correction.",
        "prompt_scaffold": "Add under a ## General Rules section"
      },
      {
        "addition": "Before dismissing a reviewer comment, carefully verify the claim. Read the actual source code to confirm whether a function delegates to another or handles logic independently. Do not assume delegation without proof.",
        "why": "Claude incorrectly dismissed a valid CodeRabbit comment by claiming a function delegated when it didn't, requiring user correction.",
        "prompt_scaffold": "Add under a ## PR Reviews section"
      },
      {
        "addition": "When modifying STATE.md or other tracking documents, preserve all existing data (velocity, counts, context) — never regenerate from scratch. After adding/removing entries, update all counts and summaries.",
        "why": "Claude regenerated STATE.md losing velocity data and failed to update counts after changes, across multiple sessions involving project state management.",
        "prompt_scaffold": "Add under a ## Project Documentation section"
      }
    ],
    "features_to_try": [
      {
        "feature": "Hooks",
        "one_liner": "Auto-run shell commands at specific lifecycle events like pre-commit or post-edit.",
        "why_for_you": "Your top friction is wrong_approach (43 instances) and buggy_code (34). A pre-commit hook that runs `git branch --show-current` and checks merge status would prevent the repeated 'committed to merged branch' issue. A post-edit hook running type-checks would catch TypeScript errors immediately.",
        "example_code": "// .claude/settings.json\n{\n  \"hooks\": {\n    \"preCommit\": [\"bash -c 'BRANCH=$(git branch --show-current); MERGED=$(git log origin/main..HEAD --oneline | wc -l); if [ $MERGED -eq 0 ] && git log origin/main --oneline | head -1 | grep -q $(git rev-parse HEAD); then echo ERROR: branch appears already merged; exit 1; fi'\"],\n    \"postEdit\": [\"npx tsc --noEmit --pretty 2>&1 | head -20\"]\n  }\n}"
      },
      {
        "feature": "Custom Skills",
        "one_liner": "Reusable prompts as markdown files triggered with a single /command.",
        "why_for_you": "You already use custom workflows like plan-phase, resolve-pr-reviews, and progress checks extensively (54 sessions on PR reviews alone). Formalizing these as skills with built-in safeguards (e.g., branch check before commit, verify all threads actually resolved) would eliminate repeated friction.",
        "example_code": "# .claude/skills/resolve-reviews/SKILL.md\n## Resolve PR Reviews\n1. Run `git branch --show-current` and `gh pr view --json state` to confirm branch is open\n2. Fetch all review threads: `gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/{pr}/comments`\n3. For each unresolved thread, read the referenced code, implement the fix\n4. After pushing, re-fetch threads and confirm resolution count matches\n5. Report any 'outside of diff range' comments separately\n6. Never report 'all resolved' without verification step"
      },
      {
        "feature": "Headless Mode",
        "one_liner": "Run Claude non-interactively from scripts and CI/CD.",
        "why_for_you": "With 13 fix_ci_failures + 6 debug_ci_failure sessions, you spend significant time on CI. Headless mode could auto-fix lint errors and common CI failures before you even look at them, and could run your progress-check/audit workflows on a schedule.",
        "example_code": "# In CI workflow (.github/workflows/autofix.yml):\n# on: [pull_request]\n# steps:\n#   - run: claude -p \"Fix all lint and type errors in this PR. Run 'npm run lint' and 'cargo clippy' to verify.\" --allowedTools \"Edit,Read,Bash,Grep\"\n\n# Local shortcut for CI debugging:\nalias fix-ci='claude -p \"Analyze the latest CI failure on this branch using gh run list and gh run view. Identify root cause and fix it.\" --allowedTools \"Edit,Read,Bash,Grep,Write\"'"
      }
    ],
    "usage_patterns": [
      {
        "title": "PR Review Resolution is Your Core Loop",
        "suggestion": "Optimize the resolve-pr-reviews workflow with guardrails since it's 54+ sessions and your primary use case.",
        "detail": "Over half your sessions involve resolving PR review comments, often across multiple rounds. The main friction points are: reporting threads as resolved when they aren't, dismissing valid comments incorrectly, and committing to merged branches. Building verification steps directly into your skill/workflow would eliminate most of this friction and reduce the rounds needed.",
        "copyable_prompt": "Resolve all open review threads on this PR. IMPORTANT: After pushing fixes, run `gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/{number}/comments` to verify thread count. Do NOT report completion until you've confirmed all threads show as resolved. Check for 'outdated' or 'outside diff range' comments too."
      },
      {
        "title": "Reduce CI Debugging Cycles",
